  "strategy": "1.Approach_and_Rationale:\nThe current approach combines several optimization components including stochastic gradient descent, coordinate ascent, simulated annealing, and crossover-based refinement. The central idea is to find a step function h that minimizes the maximum self-overlap when shifted. This approach addresses potential local optima by diversifying the search space through interval selection and multi-resolution testing. It leverages the known efficient solver madasenipm for second-order cone programming to handle constraints. The key limitations include computational intensity due to the high dimensionality and the non-convex nature of the problem, requiring careful parameter tuning and parallel processing.\n\n2.Mathematical_Specification:\n-Representation: The function h is represented as a step function with values in [0,1] at discrete grid points.\n-Objective:min C_5 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\n-Constraints:0<=h(x)<=1 and \u222bh(x)dx=0.5 (since integral over [0,2] is 1)\nDerivation: The problem requires minimizing the maximum overlap of h with its shifted versions. The approach uses a surrogate optimization technique with projected gradient descent and interval constraint handling. For optimization, a variant of projected coordinate-wise updates with line search is used for linear relaxation.\n\n3.Implementation_Procedure:\nParent validation is checked for grid feasibility and constraint satisfaction. Initialization chooses between parent-based interpolation or uniform random initialization. The core update combines projected gradient descent with simulated annealing moves to escape local optima. Any infeasible solutions are repaired by projection onto the constrained simplex. Best candidates are tracked across phases. Termination checks elapsed time and objective improvement stall. \n-Phases include:\n1. Stochastic coordinate-based coordinate descent with restarts.\n2. Iterative repair and evolution.\n3. Global search with interval annealing.\n\n4.Search_Choices_and_Variations:\n-Default resolution: 200 (good balance between search space and runtime)\n-Hyperparameter ranges:\n  learning_rate: [1e-3, 0.1] with adaptive scheduling\n  temperature: [1.0, 3.0] softening schedule\n  population_size: [2,5,10] genetic algorithm parameter\n-Adjustments based on fitness plateau trigger diversity increase\n-Resolution scaling: 100 to 1000 points (logarithmic scale by factors of 2 matched to budget)\n\n5.Computational_Budget_and_Robustness:\n-Phased execution: 30%, 40%, 30% duration allocation\n-Termination checking by phase and epoch improvement\n-Strategic fallback to parent solution on critical failure\n-Adaptive budget allocation handles rescheduling of stalled runs\n\n6.Validation_and_Return_Contract:\n-All candidates must satisfy 0<=h[i]<=1 and \u222bh(x)dx=0.5\n-Objective computed with finite-element projection and correlation.\n-Finale check with brute-force numeric confirmation\n-Detailed runtime resource usage estimation",
